#OMNISIM R2025a utf8

# A GRIPPER PICKS UP A SPONGE AND SCRUBS A DISH WITH IT.
#
# The first world in this tree in which a rigid gripper manipulates a VOLUMETRIC
# soft body and then USES it as a tool. The sponge is a tetrahedral-FEM
# `SoftBody` on newton's SolverVBD; the gripper is a five-axis prismatic gantry
# of ordinary rigid bodies; and both are owned by ONE SolverVBD, which is the
# whole reason the pinch holds (see THE SOLVER, below).
#
# Lineage: the gantry is the same 5-DoF unit as newton_vbd_cloth_grasp and
# newton_vbd_tshirt_grasp, unchanged in structure. What is new here is the
# TOOL USE -- the deformable is not the payload, it is the instrument, and the
# demo is scored on what the instrument accomplishes against a third body.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT TO EXPECT, and the proof is numeric rather than visual
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   SPONGE   a 100 x 75 x 40 mm tet-FEM block, 60 particles, resting on the
#            worktop. It is squeezed between two pads, lifted, carried over the
#            dish, pressed onto it and swept in a raster. It must DEFORM (the
#            pads close past its rest half-width and it squashes) and it must
#            TRACK the pads through the lift.
#
#   DISH     a static 200 mm plate. Its scrub surface is at z = 0.912.
#
#   GRIME    12 visual-only specks on the plate. They carry NO Solid, NO
#            boundingObject and NO Physics, so they are never registered with
#            Newton at all -- they cannot perturb the physics and the
#            "a deleted node keeps blocking rays and contacts" engine gap
#            cannot apply to them. The controller lerps each speck's baseColor
#            toward the plate's own ceramic colour as the sponge passes over.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SOLVER, and why it is "vbd" and not "mujoco+vbd"
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `newtonSolver "vbd"` puts ONE SolverVBD in charge of every rigid body, joint
# AND particle. That is the only configuration in which a gripper pad and a
# sponge particle are relaxed in the SAME solver sweep. The coupled
# "mujoco+vbd" path exchanges state over a proxy, so the particles only ever
# see a pad whose pose was synced from the PREVIOUS rigid solve -- a pinch
# resolved against a one-step-stale jaw. Both shipped deformable-grasp worlds
# are on "vbd" for this reason.
#
# ⚠ THE PRICE IS LARGE AND IS LOGGED AT `INFO`, SO --fail-on-warning WILL NOT
# CATCH ANY OF IT. There is no mj_model on this path, therefore:
#   * NO TouchSensor, NO Connector / VacuumGripper welds, NO raycast-backed
#     devices (DistanceSensor / Receiver / LightSensor / Radar / recognition
#     occlusion).
#   * NO contact readback at all -- getContactPoints, GET /sim/contacts and
#     GET /sim/grips are empty. THE GRASP CANNOT BE PROVEN BY A CONTACT QUERY
#     HERE. It is proven geometrically instead (see THE GATE, below).
#   * newtonCone / newtonImpratio / newtonIterations / newtonLsIterations /
#     newtonNoslipIterations / newtonNjmax / newtonNconmax are MuJoCo fields and
#     NONE of them is read. Deliberately absent from the WorldInfo below --
#     writing them here would be cargo cult.
#   * Rigid contact is AVBD penalty / augmented-Lagrangian, not a friction cone.
#     The only friction knob is newtonGroundMu (which also supplies
#     model.soft_contact_mu, i.e. how grippy the pads are against the sponge).
#
# ⚠ A `PositionSensor` READS 0.0 FOREVER ON THIS PATH, SILENTLY. VBD is a
# maximal-coordinate solver: it integrates body_q and never maintains joint_q,
# which is what get_joint_angle reads. Measured on a 2-axis probe: the sensor
# read 0.0 while the body moved half a metre, exit 0, PASS, no warning. There
# is therefore DELIBERATELY NO PositionSensor in this world -- every position in
# the controller comes from Supervisor.getFromDef(...).getPosition(), which
# reads body_q and is exact.
#
# ⚠ `maxForce` IS NOT A FORCE CEILING. OmniSim turns a position motor's
# maxForce into the joint's PD stiffness (targetKe = maxForce * 10, targetKd =
# maxForce * 0.5) and VBD honours those gains while IGNORING the effort limit
# itself. Read every maxForce below as a stiffness. The sizing rule is
# arithmetic and was verified to 7 significant figures on a static-sag probe:
#
#       maxForce = load_newtons / (10 * acceptable_sag_metres)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠⚠ RUN WITH  OMNISIM_CLOTH_SELF_CONTACT=0  OR THE GRIP SLIPS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Measured on the sibling t-shirt world: particle self-contact ON gives 22.11 mm
# of slip during the carry, OFF gives 0.92 mm -- a 24x difference. There is NO
# WORLD FIELD FOR THIS, only the environment variable, which is a real
# expressiveness gap in the format and is flagged as such here rather than
# quietly worked around.
#
# ⚠ Note for anyone reading commit 1fb7f135f: its message says self-contact
# "STAYS: it buys the 24x grasp-tracking improvement". That reads the same
# measurement BACKWARDS. Both deformable-grasp worlds require it OFF.
#
# The full run line, including the telemetry that is the ONLY way to read
# particle state (a SoftBody's `translation` is never written back, so a
# supervisor polling it sees the authored value forever whether the block is
# simulating perfectly or is completely inert):
#
#   OMNISIM_CLOTH_SELF_CONTACT=0 \
#   OMNISIM_CLOTH_TELEMETRY=$PWD/.build_tmp/sponge.jsonl \
#   OMNISIM_CLOTH_TELEMETRY_EVERY=10 \
#   SPONGE_LOG=$PWD/.build_tmp/sponge_pads.jsonl \
#   python -m omnisim run-headless \
#     projects/samples/demos/worlds/physics/newton_vbd_sponge_dishwash.omniworld \
#     --duration 420
#
# ⚠ ALWAYS SET `SPONGE_LOG`. The controller's default log path is inside its own
# committed .build_tmp/, and a sibling demo has already truncated a tracked file
# that way.
#
# ⚠ BUDGET REAL TIME. This is a CUDA workload (SolverVBD is a warp solver, so
# the model is forced to the GPU even though the rigid half is small). The
# sibling t-shirt demo needs ~3-4 minutes of wall clock for ~7 s of sim on an
# RTX 3060 laptop. `--duration 45` does NOT complete it. Expect a slow FIRST run
# after any runtime version bump -- that is the cold warp kernel cache, not a
# hang.
#
# ⚠ THE HEADLESS EXIT CODE IS NOT THE VERDICT for this class of demo. The
# sibling world reports `FAIL: simulator exited early with code 4294967295`
# AFTER its controller finishes and pauses the sim, while incomplete runs
# report PASS. Judge this demo by SPONGE_LOG, never by the exit code.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE GATE -- why the dish cannot be cleaned by an empty gripper
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The obvious way to write this demo is to clean a speck whenever the gripper
# passes over it. That would score the gripper's INTENT, not its achievement --
# a gripper that missed the sponge entirely would still polish the plate, and
# the demo would look perfect while proving nothing.
#
# So the scrub is gated on a MEASURED quantity: the live separation of the two
# pad Solids. Commanded closed gap is 2 * CLOSED_HALF_GAP; the sponge pushes the
# PD-driven jaws apart from it, and the excess IS the sponge. Below
# SPONGE_MIN_GAP the jaws have met and there is nothing between them, so no
# speck is cleaned no matter where the gripper goes.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE LOAD PATH -- designed around a known engine defect
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ⚠ RIGID-ON-SOFT IS BROKEN IN NEWTON. A rigid body supported ONLY by particles
# gains energy and is ejected (measured z = 0.57 .. 194 m). It reproduces in
# pure newton with OmniSim absent, in both coupled and uncoupled solvers, is not
# a timestep artifact (8x smaller dt made it worse), and the mechanism is
# unidentified. SOFT-ON-RIGID is stable and is measured to hold.
#
# Every load path in this world is therefore soft-on-rigid:
#   * the pads are held by PD joints, never by the sponge;
#   * the sponge rests on a STATIC worktop and presses onto a STATIC dish.
# Do not add a rigid body that rests on the sponge. It will launch.

EXTERNPROTO "omnisim://projects/objects/backgrounds/protos/OmniSimSky.proto"
EXTERNPROTO "omnisim://projects/objects/lights/protos/OmniSimSun.proto"
EXTERNPROTO "omnisim://projects/objects/lights/protos/OmniSimSunMarker.proto"

WorldInfo {
  gravity 9.81
  basicTimeStep 8
  coordinateSystem "ENU"
  # ⚠ Every MuJoCo tuning field is UNREAD on this path and is deliberately
  # omitted. newtonGroundMu is the exception: it is the rigid friction AND it
  # supplies model.soft_contact_mu, i.e. the pad-vs-sponge grip.
  newtonSolver "vbd"
  newtonSubsteps 10
  newtonStatics TRUE
  newtonGroundMu 3
}
Viewpoint {
  orientation -0.278945 0.120681 0.952694 2.359689
  position 0.826 -0.879 1.672
}
# ==========================================================================
# THE LIGHTING IS NOW THREE LINES. IT USED TO BE FIVE NODES.
# ==========================================================================
# ⚠ THE CANONICAL RECIPE, AND DEF SUN MUST COME FIRST. OmBackground.cpp:654-675
# derives BOTH the atmospheric sky and the baked IBL cube from the FIRST enabled
# DirectionalLight in file order and then `break`s. A fill light authored above
# this line silently hijacks the sky bake -- the sky would be lit by the fill's
# direction and the sun would become an ordinary lamp. Nothing warns.
#
# ⚠ WHAT WAS DELETED HERE, AND WHY IT WAS SAFE. This world used to carry a
# shadowless fill `DirectionalLight` AND a `PointLight` over the sink, both
# authored after DEF SUN so as not to trip the hazard above. Their stated job
# was to make the SPONGE legible, because `SoftBody` had no `appearance` field
# and a Phong surface takes no image-based fill -- so the sponge was pure black
# on every face the sun missed, and two lamps were the only available fix. The
# node gained `appearance` on 2026-08-15, the sponge is now a PBRAppearance, and
# the reason both lamps existed is gone. They are deleted rather than dimmed:
# a fill light that is no longer needed is not neutral, it costs a light slot,
# it flattens the shading it touches, and it is the thing most likely to be
# copied into the next world along with its obsolete justification.
#
# ⭐ `luminosity` IS THE REPLACEMENT AND IT IS A BETTER INSTRUMENT THAN A LAMP.
# OmPbrAppearance.cpp:419-422 hands the shader `backgroundLuminosity *
# IBLStrength` as one premultiplied float, so this single number scales the fill
# on EVERY PBR material in the world at once. Two properties a DirectionalLight
# does not have: it reaches surfaces facing ANY direction (a directional fill
# contributes exactly 0 to an underside, which is precisely where the black
# was), and on the atmospheric path the VISIBLE SKY is drawn by sky_apply.frag
# and is byte-identical at every value -- so raising it buys fill for free
# instead of blowing out the background.
#
# 3.2 rather than the old 1.8 because this is an indoor scene that has just lost
# two lamps, and the value was tuned by measurement, not by eye: see the DISH
# below, where the pair (luminosity, dish roughness) is what took the frame from
# 34.7% blown-out pixels to a plate that still reads as glazed ceramic.
#
# ⚠ The IBL cube is ACHROMATIC by construction (sky_to_cubemap.frag:181
# white-balances it to neutral grey), so this fill is grey no matter what the
# sky looks like. Anything warm in this scene has to come from a baseColor.
OmniSimSky {
  luminosity 1.6
}
DEF SUN OmniSimSun {
}
DEF SUN_MARKER OmniSimSunMarker {
}
DEF FLOOR Solid {
  translation 0 0 -0.02
  children [
    Shape {
      appearance PBRAppearance {
        baseColor 0.22 0.23 0.26
        roughness 1
        metalness 0
      }
      geometry Box {
        size 6 6 0.04
      }
    }
  ]
  name "floor"
  boundingObject Box {
    size 6 6 0.04
  }
}
# The worktop. Static (no Physics node), so it is a Newton static collider --
# statics are ON by default and newtonStatics TRUE pins them ON regardless.
# Top face at z = 0.90; that is the number the sponge's rest height is built on.
DEF WORKTOP Solid {
  translation 0 0 0.88
  children [
    Shape {
      appearance PBRAppearance {
        baseColor 0.30 0.31 0.34
        roughness 0.35
        metalness 0
      }
      geometry Box {
        size 1.1 0.62 0.04
      }
    }
  ]
  name "worktop"
  boundingObject Box {
    size 1.1 0.62 0.04
  }
}
# Splashback, visual only -- no boundingObject, so it is never registered with
# Newton and cannot snag the gantry.
DEF SPLASHBACK Solid {
  translation 0 0.32 1.10
  children [
    Shape {
      appearance PBRAppearance {
        baseColor 0.82 0.84 0.83
        roughness 0.25
        metalness 0
      }
      geometry Box {
        size 1.1 0.02 0.4
      }
    }
  ]
  name "splashback"
}
# ===========================================================================
# THE DISH. Shallow 200 mm plate, static, resting on the worktop.
#
# ⚠ VISUAL AND COLLIDER ARE DELIBERATELY DIFFERENT SHAPES. Two engine facts
# force it:
#   1. A `Cylinder` boundingObject IS SUBSTITUTED BY A CAPSULE, and for a disc
#      (half_height <= radius) the length correction is deliberately skipped --
#      so `Cylinder { radius 0.1 height 0.012 }` would collide as a 212 mm PILL,
#      an invisible bulge 17x the plate's real thickness.
#   2. Every triangle-mesh collider is silently convexified by MuJoCo, so a
#      one-piece concave plate would collide as its own hull and the sponge
#      would ride the rim.
# A Box is the one primitive that reaches the solver with its authored geometry
# AND rotation intact. Side 0.170 supports the full scrub radius (0.085) while
# never protruding past the visible disc except on the diagonals.
#
# ⛔ NEVER a `Cone` boundingObject -- GeoType.CONE is absent from newton's
# MuJoCo geom mapping and registering one kills physics for the WHOLE world.
#
# NOTE: OmniSim's Cylinder is Z-ALIGNED, so in this Z-up ENU world it lies flat
# as a disc with NO rotation. This differs from classic VRML, where Cylinder is
# Y-aligned.
#
# Plate spans z = 0.900 .. 0.912. SCRUB SURFACE IS AT z = 0.912.
# ===========================================================================
#
# ⚠ THE PLATE BLEW OUT, AND `IBLStrength` WAS THE WHOLE CAUSE. THE OBVIOUS
# SUSPECTS WERE ALL MEASURED AND ALL CLEARED.
#
# The plate was 34.7% of the frame at or past 250/255 -- a third of the image
# carrying no information. A near-white horizontal disc under a key light looks
# exactly like a specular blowout, and `roughness 0.14` (a mirror-narrow lobe)
# looks exactly like the reason. It is not. Held at one framing (150 steps,
# harness, 1896x1113) and changed one at a time:
#
#   sky luminosity   1.2 / 1.6 / 2.0        ->  7.37% / 7.65% / 7.80% saturated
#   SUN intensity    2.5 / 1.6 / 1.2 / 0.9  ->  no effect (tracked luminosity)
#   plate roughness  0.38 / 0.75            ->  7.65% / 7.65%  -- IDENTICAL
#   plate IBLStrength 1.0 / 0.5 / 0.25      ->  7.65% / 0.80% / 0.80%
#
# So it is a DIFFUSE clip from image-based light, not a specular highlight: a
# 0.94 albedo times a per-material IBL multiplier is already over 1.0 before any
# lamp is involved, which is why widening the specular lobe by 2x and dimming
# the sun to a third both did precisely nothing. The fix is one number:
#
#   IBLStrength 1.4 -> 0.5   the ONLY change that moves the measurement.
#                            0.25 is no better than 0.5, i.e. 0.5 is already
#                            below the clip, so it is the value that keeps the
#                            most light while carrying no blown pixels.
#
#   roughness 0.14 -> 0.30   kept, but labelled honestly: this was measured NOT
#                            to be the blowout, and it is here only because a
#                            glazed dinner plate is not a mirror and 0.14 will
#                            catch a hard sun glint at camera angles this
#                            framing does not happen to use.
#
# ⚠ GENERAL LESSON, since this cost a round: on a world using the canonical
# recipe, ambient is not a soft floor you can ignore -- `ambientIntensity 0`
# means IBL is ALL the fill there is, so `IBLStrength` is a first-class exposure
# control and is the first thing to sweep when something is too bright, ahead of
# the light nodes.
#
# ⛔ baseColor IS NOT AVAILABLE AS AN EXPOSURE KNOB HERE, AND THE REASON IS NOT
# IN THIS FILE. 0.94 0.945 0.93 is brighter than any real dielectric and the
# obvious fix is to drop it to a physical ~0.86 -- DO NOT. The controller
# hardcodes the plate colour as its clean endpoint:
#
#     vbd_sponge_dishwash.py:157   CLEAN_RGB = (0.94, 0.945, 0.93)
#
# and lerps every speck's baseColor between GRIME_RGB and that value as the
# sponge passes. Darken the plate and a fully-cleaned speck lands on 0.94
# against a 0.86 plate -- twelve pale discs, permanently, on a plate the demo
# has just declared clean. The two numbers are ONE number living in two files
# and the world cannot see the other half. (This also makes the authored
# baseColor on each speck below cosmetic: the controller overwrites all twelve
# every step from GRIME_RGB. Their roughness / metalness / IBLStrength are NOT
# overwritten, which is why those three do have to be maintained by hand.)
#
# ⚠ THE 12 GRIME SPECKS MUST TRACK BOTH CHANGES ABOVE. A speck is invisible at
# grime = 0 only if its material is IDENTICAL to the plate's, so roughness,
# metalness and IBLStrength are copied verbatim below, twelve times. Retune the
# plate and not the specks and a "cleaned" plate keeps twelve faintly visible
# discs -- the demo would look finished and prove nothing.
DEF DISH Solid {
  translation 0.18 0 0.906
  children [
    Shape {
      appearance PBRAppearance {
        # ⛔ PINNED to vbd_sponge_dishwash.py:157 CLEAN_RGB -- see above.
        baseColor 0.94 0.945 0.93
        roughness 0.30
        # ⚠ metalness DEFAULTS TO 1 in the schema. An inline PBRAppearance that
        # omits it is a METAL, which under ambientIntensity 0 renders near-black.
        metalness 0
        IBLStrength 0.5
      }
      geometry Cylinder {
        radius 0.1
        height 0.012
        subdivision 64
      }
    }
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # GRIME. Visual-only: Pose > Shape, with no Solid, no boundingObject and no
    # Physics anywhere, so none of this is ever registered with Newton.
    #
    # ⚠ EACH SPECK OWNS ITS OWN PBRAppearance. A `USE` would share ONE node, and
    # a single setSFColor would then clean every speck at once.
    #
    # roughness and metalness match the plate exactly, so at grime = 0 a speck
    # is indistinguishable from clean ceramic -- visually gone, still present in
    # the tree, so the demo replays after a reset.
    #
    # Specks sit at local z = +0.0065, a 0.5 mm proud of the plate face, which
    # kills z-fighting. All centres are within r = 0.075 so every one lies over
    # supported collider.
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    DEF GRIME_00 Pose {
      translation 0.021 -0.034 0.0065
      children [
        Shape {
          appearance DEF GRIME_APP_00 PBRAppearance {
            baseColor 0.34 0.26 0.14
            roughness 0.30
            metalness 0
            IBLStrength 0.5
          }
          geometry Cylinder {
            radius 0.011
            height 0.0006
            subdivision 48
          }
        }
      ]
    }
    DEF GRIME_01 Pose {
      translation -0.038 0.017 0.0065
      children [
        Shape {
          appearance DEF GRIME_APP_01 PBRAppearance {
            baseColor 0.34 0.26 0.14
            roughness 0.30
            metalness 0
            IBLStrength 0.5
          }
          geometry Cylinder {
            radius 0.013
            height 0.0006
            subdivision 48
          }
        }
      ]
    }
    DEF GRIME_02 Pose {
      translation 0.055 0.028 0.0065
      children [
        Shape {
          appearance DEF GRIME_APP_02 PBRAppearance {
            baseColor 0.31 0.24 0.13
            roughness 0.30
            metalness 0
            IBLStrength 0.5
          }
          geometry Cylinder {
            radius 0.010
            height 0.0006
            subdivision 48
          }
        }
      ]
    }
    DEF GRIME_03 Pose {
      translation -0.014 0.052 0.0065
      children [
        Shape {
          appearance DEF GRIME_APP_03 PBRAppearance {
            baseColor 0.36 0.28 0.15
            roughness 0.30
            metalness 0
            IBLStrength 0.5
          }
          geometry Cylinder {
            radius 0.012
            height 0.0006
            subdivision 48
          }
        }
      ]
    }
    DEF GRIME_04 Pose {
      translation 0.008 0.004 0.0065
      children [
        Shape {
          appearance DEF GRIME_APP_04 PBRAppearance {
            baseColor 0.29 0.22 0.12
            roughness 0.30
            metalness 0
            IBLStrength 0.5
          }
          geometry Cylinder {
            radius 0.015
            height 0.0006
            subdivision 48
          }
        }
      ]
    }
    DEF GRIME_05 Pose {
      translation -0.058 -0.031 0.0065
      children [
        Shape {
          appearance DEF GRIME_APP_05 PBRAppearance {
            baseColor 0.33 0.25 0.14
            roughness 0.30
            metalness 0
            IBLStrength 0.5
          }
          geometry Cylinder {
            radius 0.011
            height 0.0006
            subdivision 48
          }
        }
      ]
    }
    DEF GRIME_06 Pose {
      translation 0.041 -0.061 0.0065
      children [
        Shape {
          appearance DEF GRIME_APP_06 PBRAppearance {
            baseColor 0.35 0.27 0.15
            roughness 0.30
            metalness 0
            IBLStrength 0.5
          }
          geometry Cylinder {
            radius 0.009
            height 0.0006
            subdivision 48
          }
        }
      ]
    }
    DEF GRIME_07 Pose {
      translation -0.031 -0.062 0.0065
      children [
        Shape {
          appearance DEF GRIME_APP_07 PBRAppearance {
            baseColor 0.32 0.24 0.13
            roughness 0.30
            metalness 0
            IBLStrength 0.5
          }
          geometry Cylinder {
            radius 0.012
            height 0.0006
            subdivision 48
          }
        }
      ]
    }
    DEF GRIME_08 Pose {
      translation 0.066 -0.012 0.0065
      children [
        Shape {
          appearance DEF GRIME_APP_08 PBRAppearance {
            baseColor 0.30 0.23 0.12
            roughness 0.30
            metalness 0
            IBLStrength 0.5
          }
          geometry Cylinder {
            radius 0.010
            height 0.0006
            subdivision 48
          }
        }
      ]
    }
    DEF GRIME_09 Pose {
      translation -0.062 0.041 0.0065
      children [
        Shape {
          appearance DEF GRIME_APP_09 PBRAppearance {
            baseColor 0.34 0.26 0.14
            roughness 0.30
            metalness 0
            IBLStrength 0.5
          }
          geometry Cylinder {
            radius 0.009
            height 0.0006
            subdivision 48
          }
        }
      ]
    }
    DEF GRIME_10 Pose {
      translation 0.026 0.058 0.0065
      children [
        Shape {
          appearance DEF GRIME_APP_10 PBRAppearance {
            baseColor 0.31 0.24 0.13
            roughness 0.30
            metalness 0
            IBLStrength 0.5
          }
          geometry Cylinder {
            radius 0.011
            height 0.0006
            subdivision 48
          }
        }
      ]
    }
    DEF GRIME_11 Pose {
      translation -0.004 -0.070 0.0065
      children [
        Shape {
          appearance DEF GRIME_APP_11 PBRAppearance {
            baseColor 0.33 0.25 0.14
            roughness 0.30
            metalness 0
            IBLStrength 0.5
          }
          geometry Cylinder {
            radius 0.010
            height 0.0006
            subdivision 48
          }
        }
      ]
